Codex findings 9 and 10, both valid, both the same root as 5-8: the live
preview mutates panes, which handleProjectMutation also reads, normalises and
persists. Six of the last eight findings come from that one design choice.

1. Preview leaves the source pane's activeTabId pointing at an absent tab.
   handleProjectMutation normalises it to another tab. Cancel restored
   membership but not selection. Now records activeTabId in the origin snapshot
   entry and restores it if that tab still exists.
2. handleProjectMutation gives a stand-in /projects tab to a pane it would
   empty, and a pane counts as empty while its only tab previews elsewhere.
   Cancel returned the tab and kept the stand-in, creating a Projects tab from
   nothing. Stand-ins created while dragSnapshot is set are tracked in
   dragFallbackTabIds and removed on cancel.

Test lesson that nearly produced a vacuous test AGAIN: my first test covered
both fixes at once and passed with the active-tab fix REVERTED, because the
pane ended with a single tab so the selection normalised correctly anyway. A
test only isolates a selection bug if the pane keeps 2+ tabs, giving the
selection somewhere wrong to land. Split into two tests; each now verified to
fail with only its own fix reverted.

Also: manual mouse.up() in a test needs the same
'[data-dnd-dragging], [data-dnd-placeholder]' count-0 wait that dragTabAndDrop
does, or active-tab assertions hit strict-mode violations on drop-animation
artifacts.

ARCHITECTURAL NOTE for whoever picks this up. I considered replacing the
state-mutating preview with a geometry-driven drop indicator, which would
delete this whole defect class along with dragSnapshot, the restore, the
empty-pane repair, persist-on-cancel and both cancellation regressions. I did
NOT do it, for two reasons. First, measured: with handleTabDragOver disabled,
dnd-kit applies NO transform to neighbouring tabs (they stay at x=256/346/428),
so the preview cannot simply be deleted - an indicator would have to be built.
Second, a third architecture mid-review discards the review context and invites
fresh findings on unreviewed code. Flagged on the PR for a human to weigh.

